id: power-up
name: Power Up
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
powerUp: true
usageLimit: 2/round
resource: power
resourceMax: 2
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Push your power to its limits. A stack of Power until the end of your next turn:
  1(T) on every Combat Roll and a quarter more Max Capacity, twice over at two.
text: >
  Power Up Maneuver [2/Round]: Concentrate your power and push it to its limits!
  –Maneuver Type: Standard
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: N/A
  –Effect: Gain a stack of Power until the end of your next turn; if you already
  possess a stack of Power, you may remove a stack of Power before applying this
  effect. For each stack of Power, increase your Combat Rolls by 1(T) and your Max
  Capacity by 1/4 (this increase to your Max Capacity only applies for the first 2
  stacks of Power).

  Power is a Resource and you cannot possess more than 2 stacks of Power. If an
  effect would stop you from losing a stack(s) of Power, instead lose those
  stack(s) at the end of your next turn.

---

# A stack, and a clock of its own for it. "Gain a stack of Power until the end of
# your next turn" - each stack carries its own duration, which is why one gained
# this turn does not leave when one gained last turn runs out.
[on used]
power.stacks += 1;
expires(power, next-turn);

# "For each stack of Power, increase your Combat Rolls by 1(T) and your Max
# Capacity by 1/4."
#
# combatRolls fans out to the Strike, the Dodge and the Wound Roll, which is what
# the rulebook means by the term - so this is one line rather than three.
#
# The Capacity is a fraction of the whole rather than a multiplier: two stacks are
# a quarter each, which is a half, and not a quarter of a quarter more. The cap in
# the entry's brackets - "only for the first 2 stacks" - needs nothing here, since
# the Resource itself cannot go above two.
[passive]
if (power.stacks > 0) {
    combatRolls += (power.stacks * tierOfPower);
    capacity.maxFraction += (power.stacks * 0.25);
}

# --- What is not built, and why ---------------------------------------------------
#
# "If an effect would stop you from losing a stack(s) of Power, instead lose those
# stack(s) at the end of your next turn."
#
# Nothing in this system stops anybody losing a Resource: there is no way for an
# effect to say it, so there is nothing for this to answer. It is a rule about a
# rule that does not exist yet, and writing the second half of it now would be
# writing a branch nothing can ever reach. When something can hold a Resource in
# place, this is where the other half goes.
#
# The choice at the front of the effect - "you may remove a stack of Power before
# applying this effect" - is asked when the Maneuver is used, because a script has
# no way to ask a player anything. What it buys is a fresh clock: the stack you
# drop is the one that would have run out first.
